.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SS "template<uint32_t dim>
.br
class csmp::HeterogeneityAndRateAwareModel< dim >"Maartje Boons & Sally Benson (Stanford visit May 16-23, 2019), model for a layered composite\&. Material parameters are inferred from rocktype\&. The composite is a dual of averages for the high-k laminations and the w-k ones\&.

.PP
\fBModel\fP Concept

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
compute the flow rate dependent saturations in the sublayers of the composite
.IP "  \(bu" 4
end-member cases:
.IP "    \(bu" 6
viscous limit (VL) where all saturations are the same, i\&.e\&. the saturation is uniform and equal to that of the cell
.IP "    \(bu" 6
CL where pc(sw_cell_average) is the same everywhere, but the saturations in the layers reflect their pc curves these limiting cases are not directionaly dependent
.PP

.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
compute the relperms, layer by layer
.IP "\(bu" 2
apply averaging to find the horizontal (weighted mean) and vertical (harmonic mean) relative permeability pairs
.IP "\(bu" 2
since this model does drainage only, relperm effects of spontaneous imbibition can be ignored
.PP

.PP
Implementation

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
extended curve fitting and interpolation between the curves
.IP "\(bu" 2
there are only 2 flow directions: horizontal and vertical, switch occurs at 45o
.IP "\(bu" 2
the anisotropy of the absolute permeability, is treated by scaling the vertical relative permeabilities with Kv/Kh ratio
.IP "\(bu" 2
capillary pressure is also averaged, but for the vertical CL case a Brooks-Corey model is used, parameterised with the properties of the low permeability layer\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
for primary drainage, the capillary entry pressure, pd of the lowest permeability layer is used
.PP

.PP
see Maple worksheet 'BHP/Stanford-visit/IMPLEMENTATION/HeterogeneityAwareSaturationFunctions'

.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
thus far, this is a drainage only model 

.PP
an alternative capillary number: k ||grad p|| / sigma is used to get around the viscosity problem
.RE
.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
MUST WE BLEND BETWEEN HORIZONTAL AND VERTICAL FLOW?
.RE
.PP

.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
: perhaps define irreducible water saturation and use it in relperm model 

.PP
: check how this can be handled efficiently for a suite of composite rocktypes 

.PP
: think about what parameters to keep rather than using functions to compute them on the fly when needed
.RE
.PP

.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
: change FlowRateDependentLayerSaturations that it deals with horizontal and vertical flow and all funky cases
.RE
.PP

.SH "Member Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> double \fBcsmp::HeterogeneityAndRateAwareModel\fP< dim >::dpcds_Phase () const\fR [virtual]\fP"

.PP
numeric implementation of capillary pressure derivative; cap value is applied Capillary pressure - saturation derivative estimated numerically\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
the capillary pressure saturation derivative is capped to a minimum value of 10 bars/m to limit slowdown of simulator during pc spreading\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
this method is never called by Shao's FECFVM simulator as it only uses the absolute values of pc\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBTodo\fP
.RS 4
dpcdsw is zero as soon as one of the phases is immobile to avoid that CFL is influenced while capillary spreading is not possible\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Implements \fBcsmp::TwoPhaseModel< dim >\fP\&.
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> double \fBcsmp::HeterogeneityAndRateAwareModel\fP< dim >::EffectiveSaturation () const\fR [virtual]\fP"

.PP
average effective saturation as required by 2-phase model SKM: thickness-weighted average of the effective saturations in the high and the low k laminations\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
must always be called first because Seff must be communicated with the base class\&. 

.PP
this method just transfers the saturation because the effective saturation is calculated later 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Reimplemented from \fBcsmp::TwoPhaseModel< dim >\fP\&.
.SS "template<uint32_t dim> void \fBcsmp::HeterogeneityAndRateAwareModel\fP< dim >::Initialize (const \fBElement\fP< dim > & e)\fR [virtual]\fP"
Computes all relevant values for the single-valued average saturation inside of the element\&. Dependent on flow direction and kv/kh ratio, averaging and scaling is done later inside of the saturation functions\&.

.PP
Computing the magnitude of the capillary pressure gradient\&. and reading rocktype and parameters for the van Genuchten relperm model\&.

.PP
\fBAttention\fP
.RS 4
this method ignores any end-point saturations provided in the input dataset (discretised on the model)

.PP
this method only deals with horizontal or vertical laminations; oblique directions are not considered\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
